]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/153423"} "9 from 9 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "1,000 metres."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format of the match sprint was changed slightly in 1932. For the first time, the final was conducted as best two of three races. Only nine cyclists came to the mark in the first round, with the only rider who had ever seen the podium at the World Championships being " [:a {:href "/athletes/16069"} "Bruno Pellizzari"] " (ITA), third-place finisher in 1930. He survived a quarter-final race against " [:a {:href "/athletes/13877"} "Ernest Chambers"] " (GBR), despite protests. Pellizzari had nearly pushed Chambers off the track but the protest was not upheld, and Pellizzari made it to the semi-finals where he faced " [:a {:href "/athletes/16991"} "Jacques van Egmond"] " (NED) in the second of two semis. Van Egmond rode outside Pellizzari for the last 1½ laps, but outrode him on the last lap to win quite easily."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the first semi, Frenchman " [:a {:href "/athletes/13875"} "Louis Chaillot"] " faced Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/14560"} "Dunc Gray"] ". That race was very close, with Chaillot edging Gray by inches. Gray then elected to bypass the bronze medal race against Pellizzari, with US newspapers noting that Gray was saving himself for the " [:a {:href "/results/153422"} "kilometre"] ", while Dutch newspapers stated that Gray was protesting the decision in his race against Chaillot. This decision not to race would pay off, as Gray won the gold medal in the kilometre time trial."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the final, Chaillot got the first race, winning, as the " [:em {} "Los Angeles Times"] " put it, “by a gnat’s whisker,” and European sources noted that many observers felt the decision was incorrect, but no protest was lodged. In the second race, van Egmond won by a bike length. In the final race, van Egmond led out the race, with Chaillot following throughout. But Chaillot could never get by the Dutchman, who won the gold medal by a wheel. Van Egmond would return in 1933 to win the World Sprint Championships."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "R1 Rep"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/16991"} "Jacques van Egmond"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NED"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NED.png"}] "NED"]] [:td {} "13.0 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "12.2 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "12.5 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "2 (1 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/13875"} "Louis Chaillot"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} "13.0 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "12.9 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "12.8 (1 h1)"] [:td {} "1 (2 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/16069"} "Bruno Pellizzari"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "– (2 h3)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "12.5 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "– (2 h2)"] [:td {} "12.7 (1 h2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/14560"} "Dunc Gray"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/AUS"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUS.png"}] "AUS"]] [:td {} "13.2 (1 h3)